\details{
Wraps the ESPN core-v2 QBR endpoint
\code{seasons/{year}/types/2/groups/{group}/qbr/0}. It returns season-to-date
regular-season QBR for every qualified passer in the requested \code{group}
(conference / division). \code{group = 80} is the all-FBS bucket and is the
sensible default; conference group ids (e.g. \code{4} ACC, \code{8} Big 12,
\code{12} SEC, \code{21} Big Ten) also work.

Two parameters ESPN's QBR path technically accepts are deliberately
\strong{not} exposed, because neither carries data for college football:
\itemize{
\item The \code{.../weeks/{week}/qbr/...} path returns the same season aggregate
for every week -- use \code{\link[=espn_cfb_player_stats]{espn_cfb_player_stats()}} for week-resolved
passing production instead.
\item The home/away game-location split (\code{.../qbr/1}, \code{.../qbr/2}) responds
\code{200} but is empty for college football; only the total (\code{.../qbr/0})
is populated.
}

The result is wide -- one row per quarterback -- with the headline \code{qbr}
column plus the 18 component metrics ESPN derives it from. Players are
returned as ESPN athlete ids only (\code{athlete_id}) unless name resolution
is requested.

When \code{athlete_detail = TRUE} the human-readable name columns
\code{athlete_display_name}, \code{athlete_first_name}, \code{athlete_last_name},
\code{athlete_jersey}, \code{athlete_position}, and \code{athlete_position_abbreviation}
are appended. There is no bulk athlete-name catalog, so resolving names
here costs \strong{one HTTP call per quarterback} -- roughly 130 extra
requests for an all-FBS pull. It therefore defaults to \code{FALSE}. A
per-athlete fetch failure leaves that quarterback's name columns \code{NA}
rather than erroring the wrapper. To resolve names without these extra
calls, join \code{athlete_id} to another athlete source (e.g.
\code{\link[=cfbd_team_roster]{cfbd_team_roster()}}).
}
